<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nihon Shoki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">720</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Nihongi, Chronicles of Japan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nihon Shoki, or Chronicles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, completed in 720, is the second-oldest book of Japanese history after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a>. Written in classical Chinese and compiled under Prince Toneri, it narrates events from the age of the gods to the reign of Empress Jitō. It presents the imperial line as descended from the sun goddess Amaterasu and records Japan's dealings with the Korean kingdoms, including Gaya, which it calls Mimana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki.jpg" alt="Nihon Shoki" width="320" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nihonshoki_tanaka_version.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…name, written with the Chinese characters for “gods” and “way”, Shinto or kami no michi, “the way of the kami”, appears first in the <b>Nihon Shoki</b> of 720, where it distinguishes the worship of Japan’s native gods from Buddhism, the new religion that had arrived from the continent.</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan’s earliest official history, the <b>Nihon Shoki</b> of 720, was written in Classical Chinese, as were the state documents of Korea’s Joseon dynasty and Vietnam’s examinations, held in Chinese until 1919.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>These stories come from the Kojiki and the <b>Nihon Shoki</b>, the country’s first written records, set down between the 6th and 8th centuries CE after Japan had adopted Chinese characters, and they show little or no relation to what archaeology has found of Jōmon culture.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese historians saw Wa as a land of hundreds of scattered tribal communities, not the unified land with a 700-year tradition that the 8th-century <b>Nihon Shoki</b> would later describe.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The distant ancestors of the Ōtomo clan were known as masters of the royal Kume guard, and the passage reflects their pledge to serve their sovereign; the part later set to music corresponds closely to the words the edict itself quotes, as recorded in the Shoku <b>Nihongi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/umi-yukaba">Umi Yukaba</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the chronicle Shoku <b>Nihongi</b>, the Imperial Court banished En no Ozunu, of Mount Katsuragi and acclaimed for his sorcery, to Izu Ōshima after he was accused of trickery with his magic.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-26">26 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
